\begin{abstract}
Large language model (LLM)-as-a-judge evaluation corrects for judge bias and benchmark contamination independently, but sequential correction produces biased model ability estimates. We formalize this failure as an omitted variable bias (OVB) problem: when judge severity and contamination effects are both present, any estimator that models one while omitting the other yields asymptotically biased ability estimates---even when the two facets are uncorrelated. We propose the Gated Many-Facet Rasch Model (G-MFRM), which jointly calibrates model ability, item difficulty, judge severity, and contamination susceptibility through staged estimation with externally anchored per-item exposure indicators. Formal identifiability analysis proves that fixing exposure externally is both necessary and sufficient for parameter separation under standard multi-judge designs. Error propagation analysis bounds ability degradation below 6\% at realistic noise levels. In synthetic experiments ($N = 50$), G-MFRM reduces ability root mean squared error (RMSE) by 51.2\% relative to sequential estimation ($p < 0.001$). On Arena-Hard with 8 target models and 9 judges, G-MFRM identifies per-model contamination signals directionally consistent with known training data exposure and interpretable judge severity profiles, with nominally higher rank agreement ($\tau = 0.857$ vs.\ $0.786$; bootstrap $\Delta\tau$ confidence interval includes zero at this sample size). Leave-one-out cross-validation confirms improved predictive fit ($\Delta\mathrm{ELPD} = -55.7$, $|\Delta/\mathrm{SE}| = 5.9$)\footnote{Code and data are available at \url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/gated_mfrm_judge_bias-43CC/}.}.
\end{abstract}

